  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1 DESCRIPTION...\n\nAn upper level ridge axis will continue to move east of the\nforecast area this morning as a leading shortwave approaches in\nsouthwest flow aloft. Height falls and elevated instability \nwill bring scattered showers early this morning along with a \nfew rumbles of thunder. CAMs depict deeper and more widespread\ndevelopment towards the Kennebec Valley and western ME mountains\nthrough the morning hours with an initial round of moderate to\nheavy rain.\n\nA better chance for widespread showers and thunderstorms will be\npossible in the afternoon hours as better upper level forcing\ninteracts with weak surface low and trough/front. RAP soundings\nsuggest we could see widespread MUCAPE values in the 1000 to \n1500 J/kg across New Hampshire and portions of western Maine and\ngenerally lower moving east. Shear will be lacking so the severe\nthreat is low, but slow storm motions and high dewpoints/PWATs\nmay lead to some torrential downpours with localized rainfall\nrates in the 1+ inch per hour range. \n\nAs of now, CAMs are suggesting that areas that see the greatest\ncoverage of showers and storms will be over northern New \nHampshire, the western Maine mountains/foothills, and over into\nthe Kennebec River Valley and Midcoast areas. Areas under the\nheaviest cores could see localized flash flooding through the\nevening hours, especially if overlapping the morning round in\nwestern/central ME. Rain will gradually diminish through the \nevening with mostly dry conditions overnight.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2 DESCRIPTION...\n\nHot and humid conditions will persist on Sunday as we remain under \nthe influence of the Bermuda High. Partly cloudy skies will prevail \nwith high temperatures into the lower 80s across the north with \nupper 80s to near 90 degrees further to the south. Dew points will \nremain elevated into the 60s near 70, although they may begin \nlowering some late day from north to south behind a cold front. This \nfront may also bring scattered showers and thunderstorms, especially \nacross the north and over western ME. Sunday night will feature low \ntemperatures into the lower 60s in most places with most shower \nactivity coming to an end. \n\nIt will remain warm on Monday with high temperatures into the middle \nto upper 80s south of the mountains but dew points will only be into \nthe lower 60s, and therefore heat indices will not be much of a \nfactor. A series of additional cold fronts will cross through mid to \nlate week, bringing rounds of scattered showers and possible \nthunderstorms... although no day currently looks like a washout. \nTemperatures will also cool towards seasonable averages with highs \ninto the upper 70s to lower 80s. Dew points will lower as well with \novernight low temperatures into the 50s.",
